    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-9997",
    ":question/problem": "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 - 12x + 36) -5$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+36 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-12x+36)-5 & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2}-5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
