      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-140277",
      ":question/problem": "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}-6y+{3}^{2})+6$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2ay+{a}^{2}=(y-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-6y+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (y-3{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}-6y+{3}^{2})+6 & = \\\\ (y-3{)}^{2}+6 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
