      ":question/problem": "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}-4y+4)+1$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2ay+{a}^{2}=(y-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4y+4 & = \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (y-2{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}-4y+4)+1 & = \\\\ (y-2{)}^{2}+1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
