      ":question/problem": "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-8x+16)+5=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+16 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-8x+16)+5 & = \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2}+5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
